      "note": "The Greek clause is verbless and its subject is a lexical noun, so under rules 53 and 63 the supplied copula goes to the predicate, which is the comparative complement and not the adjunct παρὰ κυρίῳ; discontinuous under rule 15, headed on the adverb's own 'as' (rule 62). REV.9.7 is the precedent where an adjunct prepositional phrase and a comparative stand together, and the corpus puts a supplied copula on ὡς in nine units (1PE.1.24, REV.1.14, REV.1.16, REV.9.5, REV.9.7, REV.9.9, REV.9.17, REV.10.1, REV.20.8) and on παρά in none. Carrying it on παρά remains defensible, and 1PE.1.24 and REV.20.8 are likewise medium."
